cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "A delta^2-L^3 Sensitivity Law for Finite-Window Weil Positivity, with a Function-Field Control of the Connes-van Suijlekom Pipeline"
abstract: >-
  A quantitative sensitivity analysis of the finite-window Weil quadratic form used in the
  Connes-van Suijlekom and Connes-Consani-Moscovici programs, calibrated against curves over
  finite fields, where the window form is the Hallouin-Perret Frobenius Gram matrix and the
  Riemann hypothesis is Weil's theorem. Proves a delta^2 sensitivity law with an L^3
  coefficient in both settings, and runs the full minimiser pipeline on deliberately planted
  off-circle spectra. The current version adds a computer-assisted certificate (Section 6.1,
  Theorem 11): for an explicit four-mode window at L = 4/5 and height gamma = 14, the
  finite-window Weil form is positive on the actual zero set and strictly negative once an
  artificial quartet of zeros is added at displacement delta in [1/4, 49/100], proved by
  exact rational interval arithmetic over 256 covering cells and reimplemented independently
  at delta = 2/5. The added quartet is a counterfactual; this places no restriction on the
  location of any actual zero. Contains no proof or advance of the Riemann hypothesis for
  zeta.
